      "blockId": "paragraph:overview:0011",
      "type": "sentence",
      "source": "You can access a composite’s underlying attributes using namespaced key paths and property-like setters and getters, as the following example demonstrates:",
      "normalizedSource": "You can access a composite’s underlying attributes using namespaced key paths and property-like setters and getters, as the following example demonstrates:",
      "sourceHash": "a30eb924e2dc11f50aa0d1659b2f0049463082544526bdecf31897fa8e77fe4a",
      "translatable": true,
      "contextBefore": "However, if you need to create composites dynamically at runtime, create an instance of this class and populate its [`elements`](nscompositeattributedescription/elements.md) property with the necessary attribute descriptions.",
      "contextAfter": "```swift\n// A model class that represents the Quake entity.\nclass Quake: NSManagedObject {\n    @NSManaged var code: String?\n    @NSManaged var place: String?\n    @NSManaged var date: Date?\n\n    // A composite attribute that uses a dictionary for its in-memory storage.\n    @NSManaged var magnitude: [String: Any]?\n}\n\n// Use namespaced key paths to access a composite's indvidual attributes.\nlet request = NSFetchRequest(entityName: \"Quake\")\nrequest.predicate = NSPredicate(format: \"magnitude.richter > 4.5\")\n\n// Use property-like setters and getters to manage the underlying attributes directly.\nquake.magnitude.richter = 4.6\nprint(quake.magnitude.richter)\n\n```",
      "documentTitle": "NSCompositeAttributeDescription",
      "framework": "CoreData",
      "ko": "",
